SNMP credential storage and upgrade safety
==========================================

Core credential creation and updates accept SNMP community strings up to 512
characters. These values are encrypted before storage. The previous varchar(255)
column could not hold many accepted encrypted values: 128 ASCII bytes, 64
2-byte UTF-8 characters or 32 4-byte UTF-8 characters already yield a 268-character
Fernet token. Requests passed validation but failed with a PostgreSQL write error.

The core Credential.snmp_community column is now Text, like other encrypted
credential columns. The plaintext API limit, encryption algorithm, key derivation,
nullable/empty/omitted input behavior, permissions and response redaction remain
unchanged. No existing values are decrypted, re-encrypted, rewritten or repaired.
The collector module has a separate SNMP configuration model and is unchanged.
Persistence of an accepted string does not establish a device's protocol limits.

Existing installations run Alembic revision 021_credential_snmp_text after
020_inapp_event_outbox. The upgrade widens the column in place and preserves
existing ciphertext, legacy plaintext, null values, metadata and row identity.
Fresh installations receive the same Text column from current ORM metadata.
Run upgrades in a maintenance window with a verified database backup and a usable
vault/key recovery procedure. ALTER TABLE takes a database lock; no zero-downtime
or large-production-database timing guarantee is made here.

Downgrading to revision 020 locks the credential table before checking ALL stored
values, including inactive and soft-deleted rows. Any value longer than 255
characters makes the downgrade fail and roll back, preserving the schema revision
and every value. Do not truncate tokens to bypass this guard. Preserve a tested
vault and reconcile the affected credentials deliberately before attempting a
rollback. Short compatible values, including 255-character Unicode legacy data,
can return to varchar(255) without losing bytes. Concurrent writes cannot slip
between the downgrade capacity check and its type change.

Qualification includes real PostgreSQL request/write/follow-up-read checks,
legacy frozen-schema upgrades, safe downgrade/re-upgrade, refused downgrades and
a competing writer. Encrypted core archives exercise long ASCII and Unicode
communities; vault restoration re-encrypts under a different test instance key,
while config-only restoration preserves the destination's existing secret.
Authentication is synthetic and hardware is not contacted. Core archive checks
do not qualify a complete customer recovery or every backup contributor.
Independent loopback HTTP probes and shipping-image gates retain separate
receipts; missing shipping results must never be inferred from local success.
